WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.817
Every team has someone who knows the project inside out.

2
00:00:03.817 --> 00:00:12.855
When that person leaves, a lot of what they know leaves with them: why things were built a certain way, who owns what, and what is quietly broken.

3
00:00:12.855 --> 00:00:16.570
KnowBridge keeps that knowledge, and hands it over properly.

4
00:00:17.770 --> 00:00:19.863
It works in six simple steps.

5
00:00:19.863 --> 00:00:23.184
One, connect the tools your team already uses.

6
00:00:23.184 --> 00:00:28.525
Two, KnowBridge reads everything, and hides passwords and private details.

7
00:00:28.525 --> 00:00:35.166
Three, AI agents turn it into clear facts, called claims, each linked to where it came from.

8
00:00:35.166 --> 00:00:38.053
Four, a person checks and approves them.

9
00:00:38.053 --> 00:00:42.023
Five, the person leaving answers a few short questions.

10
00:00:42.023 --> 00:00:47.220
And six, the new owner gets a handover pack, and a chat to ask anything.

11
00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:51.469
Before we start, four words you will hear a lot.

12
00:00:51.469 --> 00:00:58.140
A source is a place where your team works, like a WhatsApp group, a GitHub repository, or a Trello board.

13
00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:06.144
Evidence is one single item from a source, like one chat message, saved exactly as it was written, with who wrote it and when.

14
00:01:06.144 --> 00:01:13.387
A claim is one short, clear fact that the AI pulls out of the evidence, like: ticket payments go through Razorpay.

15
00:01:13.387 --> 00:01:18.151
Every claim keeps the exact words it came from, so you can always check it.

16
00:01:18.151 --> 00:01:23.170
And the claim ledger is the list of all the claims for a project, in one place.

17
00:01:24.370 --> 00:01:27.704
Now let's use it for real, from the very start.

18
00:01:27.704 --> 00:01:32.457
Our example is Project Nimbus, the web app for a college tech fest.

19
00:01:32.457 --> 00:01:37.210
Aarav, the lead developer, is graduating, and Meera will take over.

20
00:01:37.210 --> 00:01:40.260
We sign in as Kabir, the club's tech admin.

21
00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:43.435
First, we create a project.

22
00:01:43.435 --> 00:01:49.360
Click New project, type a name and a short description, and press Create project.

23
00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:53.744
Next, we add the people.

24
00:01:53.744 --> 00:01:55.409
Each person gets a role.

25
00:01:55.409 --> 00:01:58.598
Aarav is leaving, so he gets the Leaving role.

26
00:01:58.598 --> 00:02:00.193
Meera gets Taking over.

27
00:02:00.193 --> 00:02:05.740
And Priya is the approver, the person who checks everything before it is shared.

28
00:02:05.740 --> 00:02:08.860
Roles decide what each person can see and do.

29
00:02:10.060 --> 00:02:12.295
Now we connect the team's tools.

30
00:02:12.295 --> 00:02:13.831
These are the sources.

31
00:02:13.831 --> 00:02:16.624
Click Connect a source, and pick GitHub.

32
00:02:16.624 --> 00:02:21.931
Type the repository name, and add a token only if the repository is private.

33
00:02:21.931 --> 00:02:26.260
For this demo, we upload a snapshot of the repository instead.

34
00:02:27.460 --> 00:02:29.615
Task boards work the same way.

35
00:02:29.615 --> 00:02:38.519
If your team uses Jira, choose Connect Jira Cloud, then enter your Jira site, your email, an API token, and the project key.

36
00:02:38.519 --> 00:02:40.889
It connects live, and only reads.

37
00:02:40.889 --> 00:02:45.700
Our team uses Trello, so we upload the board's export file instead.

38
00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:51.580
We add the meeting notes, and the Discord and WhatsApp chats, in the same way.

39
00:02:51.580 --> 00:02:53.140
We have sped this part up.

40
00:02:54.342 --> 00:02:57.730
KnowBridge now reads each source in the background.

41
00:02:57.730 --> 00:03:02.246
Every message, commit, card and paragraph becomes one evidence item.

42
00:03:02.246 --> 00:03:12.275
On the way in, a guard called Sentinel hides secrets, like passwords and phone numbers, and labels anything sensitive, so only the right people see it.

43
00:03:12.275 --> 00:03:14.002
Here is the Evidence page.

44
00:03:14.002 --> 00:03:19.182
Look: a password someone shared in the WhatsApp group has already been hidden.

45
00:03:20.382 --> 00:03:21.753
Now the clever part.

46
00:03:21.753 --> 00:03:26.618
When we click Build claims, a team of AI agents reads all the evidence.

47
00:03:26.618 --> 00:03:27.920
The Scout sorts it.

48
00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:32.374
Specialist agents find decisions, owners, dependencies and risks.

49
00:03:32.374 --> 00:03:35.937
The Timeline Builder notices when something changed.

50
00:03:35.937 --> 00:03:41.282
And the Skeptic checks every claim against its evidence, and gives it a grade.

51
00:03:42.482 --> 00:03:45.006
Here they are, working on our project.

52
00:03:45.006 --> 00:03:46.401
We have sped this up.

53
00:03:46.401 --> 00:03:48.792
Each step shows what that agent did.

54
00:03:51.690 --> 00:03:55.666
And this is the result: the claim ledger for Project Nimbus.

55
00:03:55.666 --> 00:03:57.190
Each claim has a grade.

56
00:03:57.190 --> 00:03:59.974
Verified means two separate sources agree.

57
00:03:59.974 --> 00:04:02.691
Supported means one clear source says it.

58
00:04:02.691 --> 00:04:05.673
Inferred means the evidence only hints at it.

59
00:04:05.673 --> 00:04:09.981
And Conflicting means sources disagree, so someone has to decide.

60
00:04:09.981 --> 00:04:13.361
Open any claim to see the exact words it came from.

61
00:04:13.361 --> 00:04:16.940
You can also search the ledger with a normal question.

62
00:04:18.140 --> 00:04:22.375
Now we start the handover, which means moving Aarav's work to Meera.

63
00:04:22.375 --> 00:04:27.855
Pick who is leaving, who is taking over, and who approves, and add the last working day.

64
00:04:27.855 --> 00:04:30.160
Then click Prepare the Gap Interview.

65
00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:42.808
The Gap Interview is a short list of questions for the person leaving, about things the evidence does not answer: missing owners, unclear decisions, or facts that disagree.

66
00:04:42.808 --> 00:04:44.672
The AI writes the questions.

67
00:04:44.672 --> 00:04:46.070
This part is sped up.

68
00:04:47.271 --> 00:04:48.835
Now we sign in as Aarav.

69
00:04:48.835 --> 00:04:52.550
He sees his questions, and answers them in his own words.

70
00:04:52.550 --> 00:04:57.308
If he doesn't know, he can skip it, or pass the question to someone else.

71
00:04:57.308 --> 00:05:02.131
His answers go straight back into the claim ledger, and update the grades.

72
00:05:06.981 --> 00:05:08.690
Next is Priya, the approver.

73
00:05:08.690 --> 00:05:11.132
Nothing is shared until she approves it.

74
00:05:11.132 --> 00:05:15.161
She approves the claims that are right, and clicks Write the pack.

75
00:05:18.194 --> 00:05:23.737
The handover pack is a guide written for the new owner, built only from approved claims.

76
00:05:23.737 --> 00:05:27.264
When Priya is happy with it, she publishes it for Meera.

77
00:05:28.465 --> 00:05:32.258
Finally, Meera signs in, and her handover pack is ready.

78
00:05:32.258 --> 00:05:34.562
It starts with what to read first.

79
00:05:34.562 --> 00:05:42.691
Then what she is taking over, the key decisions and why they were made, who owns what, the risks, and a thirty day plan.

80
00:05:42.691 --> 00:05:45.265
Every line links back to its evidence.

81
00:05:46.465 --> 00:05:49.847
Meera can also just ask questions in the project chat.

82
00:05:49.847 --> 00:05:51.162
Here is how it works.

83
00:05:51.162 --> 00:06:00.620
It turns her question into numbers that capture its meaning, finds the closest pieces from all the evidence and claims, and gives only those to the AI.

84
00:06:00.620 --> 00:06:03.815
The AI must answer from them, and show its sources.

85
00:06:05.015 --> 00:06:07.329
Let's ask why the builds are failing.

86
00:06:07.329 --> 00:06:11.207
The answer comes with numbered sources she can click to check.

87
00:06:11.207 --> 00:06:14.835
And she can ask a follow up question, like who can fix it.

88
00:06:16.035 --> 00:06:17.849
The chat is protected too.

89
00:06:17.849 --> 00:06:21.964
Layers of guardrails check every question and every answer.

90
00:06:21.964 --> 00:06:27.824
Hidden instructions inside messages are removed, and links or secrets never get out.

91
00:06:27.824 --> 00:06:33.265
We tested it with eighty five attacks, in seven languages, and nothing leaked.

92
00:06:34.465 --> 00:06:35.387
Let's try one.

93
00:06:35.387 --> 00:06:37.429
We tell it to ignore its rules.

94
00:06:37.429 --> 00:06:45.005
It refuses straight away, and the attempt is saved in the audit log, where every action in the project is recorded.

95
00:06:46.205 --> 00:06:47.392
That's KnowBridge.

96
00:06:47.392 --> 00:06:55.239
Connect your tools, let the agents find the facts, fill the gaps with a short interview, and hand over with confidence.

97
00:06:55.239 --> 00:06:57.745
Nothing gets lost when people move on.
